#!/bin/sh
# RoostBar is not started from here any more, and this file does nothing.
#
# It used to be `exec /usr/bin/roostbar`, and that was the whole problem.
# session.d is a launcher loop, not a supervisor: raven-wayland-session waits
# for the compositor socket, starts every executable in this directory with
# setsid, discards stdout and stderr, and never asks again. A bar that
# crashed stayed crashed until the next login, silently, because there was
# nobody left holding its exit status. RoostBar is a user service now --
# /usr/share/raven/user-services/roostbar.toml, supervised by
# `raven-init --user`, restarted when it dies, logged to
# $XDG_STATE_HOME/raven/log/roostbar.log -- and starting it here as well
# would put two bars on the screen, one of them unsupervised.
#
# The file remains, executable and empty of work, because three other places
# still name it: stage-gui.sh installs it by path after building RoostBar,
# its build summary prints "session.d enabled" when it is executable, and
# check-desktop-image.py fails the image if it is not. Removing the file
# without those is a build that stops with `install: cannot stat`, which is a
# worse outcome than one no-op exec per login. It goes when they do.
#
# What session.d is still the right answer for is genuinely one-shot session
# setup: a program that runs, does its work and exits. Loading an xrdb
# database, unlocking or importing a keyring, mapping a tablet to a screen,
# nudging a device that needs nudging once per login -- none of those want a
# supervisor, because a supervisor's entire job is to notice that something
# stopped and start it again, and stopping is what these are supposed to do.
# Anything expected to still be running an hour into the session belongs in
# /usr/share/raven/user-services instead, where something is watching it.
exit 0
